# G10 (Active Redteam) — Documented, Not Wired

G10 would have the 8 redteam gates actually fire periodically on the running server
and update the dynamic graph with probe results. The gates are documented in
mcp_graph.oo and the dispatch surface is reachable via the audit tier. Implementation
is out of scope for this pass; the next-sprint candidate.

# Removed Obsolete Paths

Before                                            After
- mcp_take_json_object (hand-rolled)              -> shared LSP table parser
- 256-message hard cap in mcp_process_stream       -> unbounded loop
- mcp_codex_embedded (47-line text blob)           -> mcp_codex_gold_master_sha (real SHA)
- 5-path heuristic probe                           -> 2-path deterministic
- safe_merge_mcp_json (170 lines bash/awk)         -> ooda mcp-merge (158 lines of .oo)
- Safe-MergeMcpJson (118 lines of PS)              -> ooda mcp-merge (same .oo)
- prompts/list advertised but no prompts/get       -> prompts/get implemented
- sampling/createMessage not offered               -> offered, returns -32605
- roots/list not offered                           -> offered
- notifications/roots/list_changed not handled     -> handled, roots stored on McpSession
- Dynamic graph state lost on restart              -> mcp_dynamic_load on boot (now real)
- Per-instance dynamic graph                       -> mcp_gossip_merge dedups across instances
- Request-reply only                               -> notifications/progress streaming + cancel
- Self-mutate by value parameter                   -> Result<String, Int> return
- Copy-update without effect                       -> Build new list, replace
- 5 unused mcp_cap_* functions                     -> removed
- mmp_stream_cancel_envelope (typo + dead)         -> removed

# Known Follow-ups (Out of Scope)

1. The Unix-socket builtins (unix_bind, unix_accept, unix_read, unix_write, unix_close) must
   be exposed by the runtime layer.
2. G10 active redteam probes are documented but not wired.
3. The gossip protocol today is local (in-process); a true cross-server gossip
   would require a transport (UDP/Unix-socket) on top of McpGossipState.
4. The dynamic graph loader uses a focused JSON parser; a general-purpose JSON
   parser would be the next step for arbitrary config-file loading.
5. R7 cap audit uses a 5-bit field; the 14 tokens can be encoded in 14 bits if needed.
